      "provenance": {
        "notes": "Spearmint only; peppermint and the many other Mentha species have their own care. Pennyroyal (Mentha pulegium) is listed by RHS as harmful if eaten. Spreads by runners: NC State, RHS and Illinois Extension all say grow it in a pot or contain the roots. Dies back in winter and regrows (RHS guide), with no numbers for the rest, so the factor is unknown; the pot can be brought indoors to a sunny windowsill. Watering: NC State moist, rich soil and poor in dry soil; RHS moist and water pots in summer; guide evenly moist. Size: NC State 1 to 2 ft both ways (stored); RHS up to 100 cm tall and wide, and 1 to 1.5 m spread in the table: it grows bigger in the ground. Toxicity: ASPCA covers the genus Mentha (essential oils; vomiting and diarrhoea after large amounts) and calls it toxic to dogs and cats; NC State rates humans low severity (same oils, same symptoms in quantity) and tags it a problem for cats and dogs. Graded 1 (agent mapping; 01c said mild). Humans eat it, so no child line is stored. Illinois Extension page read: nothing on toxicity or watering."
      },
